<p>A Paris court found French cement maker Lafarge guilty of financing armed groups in Syria, including Islamic State. The court fined the company €1.125 million and sentenced former chief executive Bruno Lafont to six years in prison; eight former employees were also convicted. The case centers on nearly €5.6 million in payments made in 2013 and 2014 through Lafarge Cement Syria and intermediaries to keep the company’s Jalabiya plant running during Syria’s war, despite sanctions and the rising power of jihadist groups. The ruling matters because French judges directly tied Lafarge’s hidden payment system to the strengthening of Islamic State and other groups as they seized resources and carried out attacks in the region and abroad, especially in Europe. It is also a notable moment for accountability over corporate conduct in conflict zones, coming after Lafarge’s 2022 guilty plea in the United States and a separate $778 million penalty for providing material support to designated terrorist organizations.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/b22VEYarj8PWl5WuWpPn7FVdxAuZoqWhmRn70ROz3bo-ixI7etK5wWuvMMBmGcMvEX68l-5OozFWOrcaFBNZ59E9ke4NSsrG2VMhT-AQJrSqHOC10odn6eSjLJ9vAxcdGYoTA59v6peNLehYQTlTYHwcQtOjoioVcb1z-rEZEQjauLY' alt='Former Lafarge CEO Bruno Lafont arrives at the Paris court, April 13, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Sanctions finding: The Paris verdict also found that Lafarge’s Syrian subsidiary had violated European sanctions.</li><li>Legal first: The case was described as the first in France to prosecute a company for financing terrorism.</li><li>Plant scale: Lafarge’s Jalabiya cement factory had cost about $680 million to build before Syria’s war.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Paris criminal court: The court viewed Lafarge’s arrangement as a &#x27;commercial partnership&#x27; with terrorist groups, not as an unavoidable cost of operating in a war zone. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/france/article/2026/04/14/french-court-condemns-organized-opaque-and-illegal-terrorist-funding-system-in-lafarge-trial_6752386_7.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Prosecutors: French prosecutors asked the court to impose the maximum corporate fine on Lafarge, and the judges did so. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1991465/french-court-jails-cement-firms-ex-ceo-over-is-funding'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Bruno Lafont: Through his lawyer, Lafarge’s former chief executive said he would appeal the ruling. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1991465/french-court-jails-cement-firms-ex-ceo-over-is-funding'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/france/article/2026/04/14/french-court-condemns-organized-opaque-and-illegal-terrorist-funding-system-in-lafarge-trial_6752386_7.html'>French court condemns &#x27;organized, opaque and illegal&#x27; terrorist funding system in Lafarge trial</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/13/french-cement-maker-lafarge-convicted-financing-terror-groups-syria'>French cement maker convicted of financing terror groups to keep its Syria plant working</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1991465/french-court-jails-cement-firms-ex-ceo-over-is-funding'>French court jails cement firm’s ex-CEO over IS ‘funding’</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/13/french-cement-giant-lafarge-guilty-of-financing-isil-in-syria'>French cement giant Lafarge guilty of financing ISIL in Syria</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-french-cement-maker-holcims-lafarge-unit-found-guilty-of-financing/'>French cement maker Holcim&#x27;s Lafarge unit found guilty of financing jihadists in Syria</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li></ul>